  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 AM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nBlustery northwesterly winds have begun to overspread the region as \nthe cold front associated with a deepening 1001 mb surface low over \nOhio has moved through. Temperatures behind the front have also \nfallen decently, with some places in western Kentucky experiencing \ngreater than 8 degree changes over the past three hours. The region \nis currently under a 30-40 kt LLJ, and while cloud cover and the \nnocturnal timing has impacted mixing, the sharp pressure gradient \nand pressure rises of around 1 mb/hr will allow for 20-25 mph \n(localized gusts > 25 mph) gusts through the rest of the night.\n\nBy daybreak Thursday, the pressure gradient overhead will begin to \nbe less pronounced, allowing for a reprieve from the gusty winds. \nHowever, this will not last long as vertical mixing deepens \nthroughout the day. 850 mb winds per the latest REFS remains in the \n20-30 mph range, so with the decent mixing in place, expect 10-15 \nmph sustained winds and 20-30 mph wind gusts. With the combination \nof wrap around moisture from the low, steep low-level lapse rates, \nand another shortwave impulse swinging over the region, chances for \nscattered rain showers will increase Thursday afternoon, especially \nfor places along and east of I-65. Enough wrap around moisture will \nstill be in place to allow for chances of isolated to widely \nscattered showers Thursday night-Frdiay. While chances are not high, \nthere is the potential for a few snow flakes to mix in with the \nshowers that pass through Friday morning and Friday evening. \n\nLooking at the synoptic pattern late Thursday and beyond, the \npreviously mentioned surface low shifts towards the NE CONUS as a \nbroad upper-level low situates itself over southern Ontario/Quebec \nand the Great Lakes region. Multiple impulses around the upper low \nwill swing over the Ohio Valley, reinforcing cold air overhead going \ninto the weekend. While temperatures Friday morning will not be the \ncoldest of the forecast period (mid to upper 30s), the afternoon \nhighs will be, with temperatures struggling to get out of the mid \n40s. Persistent cloud cover begins to retreat Friday night, allowing \nfor decent radiational cooling to occur. From the latest LREF, \nprobabilities of below freezing temperatures Saturday and Sunday \nmorning are greater than 70%, decreasing to the 40-70% range for \nMonday morning. Afternoon highs will struggle to get above 50 during \nthe weekend. The best chances will be south of I-64 and west of I-65 \non Sunday where there is a 50-70% probability (chances quickly drop \noff to the north and east.\n\nWith high pressure building in, especially late Friday and beyond, \nanother dry stretch of weather will be in store for the Ohio Valley. \nBy the end of the forecast period on Wednesday, the region will be \nunder a 1032 mb surface high (~95th percentile climatologically) and \na 578 dm upper-level ridge (>90th percentile climatologically), \nexpected to persist well into next week. Temperatures will begin to \nmoderate some, with afternoon highs closer to normal for this time \nof year in the 50s. Decent radiational cooling will keep overnight \ntemperatures cold though, with near/below freezing lows possible \nthrough next Wednesday.",
